  {
   "id": "jdqbUqapCaAhxMyciWqBX",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -761.6754264527079,
   "width": 795.6500244140625,
   "height": 375,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#374151",
   "backgroundColor": "#96f2d7",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 1,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "index": "b2l",
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 590636936,
   "version": 351,
   "versionNonce": 1844589524,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": [],
   "updated": 1792905550769,
   "created": 1792847088749,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "- Splat-and-Seek is a hardware/software localization core for PolarFire SoC.\n- The site is surveyed once with a camera and reconstructed offline into a\n  3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) map, a photorealistic model that doubles as\n  the inspection record.\n- On the robot, we run Monte Carlo localization: 300 candidate poses (x, y,\n  heading) are rendered from the map and scored against the live camera\n  frame.\n- A custom FPGA engine streams one depth-sorted set of Gaussians from\n  on-chip LSRAM to four parallel pose lanes, renders each candidate as a\n  32×24 colour-and-depth image, and computes a fixed-point match score.\n- A real-time RISC-V hart (AMP) runs the particle filter, fusing these\n  scores with wheel odometry. Linux on the other cores handles the map,\n  camera driver and networking, with DMA over the FIC0 AXI4 interface.\n- The engine is sized for the MPFS250T: about 45,000 Gaussians in LSRAM and\n  under half of the 784 math blocks.",
   "fontSize": 20,
   "baseFontSize": null,
   "fontFamily": 5,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "originalText": "- Splat-and-Seek is a hardware/software localization core for PolarFire SoC.\n- The site is surveyed once with a camera and reconstructed offline into a\n  3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) map, a photorealistic model that doubles as\n  the inspection record.\n- On the robot, we run Monte Carlo localization: 300 candidate poses (x, y,\n  heading) are rendered from the map and scored against the live camera\n  frame.\n- A custom FPGA engine streams one depth-sorted set of Gaussians from\n  on-chip LSRAM to four parallel pose lanes, renders each candidate as a\n  32×24 colour-and-depth image, and computes a fixed-point match score.\n- A real-time RISC-V hart (AMP) runs the particle filter, fusing these\n  scores with wheel odometry. Linux on the other cores handles the map,\n  camera driver and networking, with DMA over the FIC0 AXI4 interface.\n- The engine is sized for the MPFS250T: about 45,000 Gaussians in LSRAM and\n  under half of the 784 math blocks.",
   "autoResize": true,
   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "labelPosition": null
  },
